  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAn upper-level low remains situated over the Great Lakes region\ntoday with the occluded surface low right along the\nOntario/Quebec border. A compact mid-level shortwave trough is \nworking its way around western edge of the upper low and will\napproach the forecast from the north this evening. Paired with\nthe left exit region of a jet streak over the western Great\nLakes and we have solid forcing for ascent and pretty good\nconfidence in timing and areal extent of precipitation this\nevening into early tonight.\n\nWe start with scattered showers and storms areawide this \nafternoon as MLCAPE exceeds 250 J/kg underneath the jet streak\nforcing. The impending shortwave will focus a better period of\nthunderstorm development roughly along and northeast of I-80 in\nNortheast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania between 7 PM and 12\nAM. This is where MLCAPE will be greatest (>500 J/kg) and\nexists the chance for organized convection that will have the\npotential for lightning, gusty winds (most likely in the 40-50 \nmph range), small hail, and heavy rain.\n\nWhile the mid-level speed max will be offset to the southwest,\neffective bulk shear of around 25-35 knots should be sufficient\nfor an organized convection. Deep layer shear should be enough\nto produce strong winds but lack of DCAPE and waning low-level\nlapse rates is likely to make 40-50 mph gusts more common rather\nthan 58+ mph (severe thunderstorm warning criteria). Small hail\nis likely to be pretty common in most thunderstorms this\nevening, aided by low freezing heights around 8-9kft and modest\nmid-level lapse rates. Large hail at or exceeding 1\" is unlikely\nbut possible with any rotating updrafts.\n\nHeavy rain is likely with thunderstorms with minor flooding\npossible is low-lying or flood-prone areas. Precipitation is\nunlikely to reach amounts for flash flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2.. \nBelow normal temperatures continue through at least Tuesday with\nlake effect rain showers continuing tonight through Tuesday\nmorning. Rain is likely to be more more persistent in Northwest\nPennsylvania, compared to the \"snowbelt\" region of Northeast\nOhio where rain showers will be more intermittent.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nTemperatures and humidity build back to near normal Wednesday\nand Thursday ahead of our next upper-level trough and low\npressure system. Scattered showers and thunderstorms are\nexpected Wednesday night into Thursday with PoPs peaking in the\n40-60% range. Can't completely rule out severe weather, but at\nthis point most storms look to remain sub-severe.",
